+## Near-Neighbor Search with symdel
+
+Symmetric deletion lookup finds every pair of CDR3 sequences within a given edit distance without building a full pairwise distance matrix. It indexes each sequence by its set of single-character deletions, so two sequences within one edit share a deletion variant and collide in the index. Cost scales with the number of sequences rather than with their square.
+
+By default `runSymdelNeighbors()` adds a `symdel_degree` column giving the number of neighbors each cell's CDR3 has. High degree marks sequences sitting in dense similarity neighborhoods, the signature of convergent recombination and antigen-driven expansion:
+
+```{r}
+scRep_example <- runSymdelNeighbors(
+  scRep_example,
+  chains = "TRB",
+  max_edits = 1
+)
+
+table(scRep_example$symdel_degree, useNA = "ifany")
+```
+
+Setting `return_object = FALSE` returns the neighbor pairs themselves, which is the more useful form when building a similarity graph:
+
+```{r}
+edges <- runSymdelNeighbors(
+  scRep_example,
+  chains = "TRB",
+  max_edits = 2,
+  return_object = FALSE
+)
+
+head(edges)
+```
+
+The edge list feeds directly into a graph representation of the repertoire:
+
+```{r}
+library(igraph)
+
+g <- graph_from_data_frame(edges[, c("from_seq", "to_seq")], directed = FALSE)
+components <- components(g)
+
+cat("Connected components:", components$no, "\n")
+cat("Largest component:", max(components$csize), "sequences\n")
+```
+
+This is the same algorithm benchmarked in the [XT-neighbor](https://github.com/heartnetkung/XT-neighbor) work. immLynx uses the CPU implementation from pyrepseq, so no GPU is required.
