+## Deep Generative Featurization with DeepTCR
+
+DeepTCR learns a latent representation of CDR3 sequences with a variational autoencoder. Where ESM-2 brings general protein knowledge learned from UniProt, the DeepTCR VAE trains on the repertoire in front of it, so its features describe the structure of that specific dataset. The two are complementary rather than interchangeable.
+
+DeepTCR prunes uninformative latent dimensions during training, so the result may be narrower than `latent_dim` requests:
+
+```{r}
+scRep_example <- runDeepTCR(
+  scRep_example,
+  chains = "TRB",
+  latent_dim = 64,
+  verbose = FALSE
+)
+
+dim(Embeddings(scRep_example, "tcr_deeptcr"))
+```
+
+Passing `use_genes = TRUE` includes V and J gene usage alongside the CDR3 sequence, which helps when gene usage carries specificity signal the CDR3 alone does not:
+
+```{r}
+scRep_example <- runDeepTCR(
+  scRep_example,
+  chains = "TRB",
+  use_genes = TRUE,
+  reduction_name = "tcr_deeptcr_genes"
+)
+```
+
+### Comparing DeepTCR and ESM-2 Representations
+
+Because both write to a dimensional reduction, they live in the same object and can be projected side by side:
+
+```{r}
+scRep_example <- runEmbeddings(scRep_example, chains = "TRB",
+                               reduction_name = "tcr_esm")
+scRep_example <- runDeepTCR(scRep_example, chains = "TRB",
+                            latent_dim = 64, verbose = FALSE)
+
+scRep_example <- RunUMAP(scRep_example, reduction = "tcr_esm",
+                         dims = 1:64, reduction.name = "umap_esm")
+scRep_example <- RunUMAP(scRep_example, reduction = "tcr_deeptcr",
+                         dims = 1:64, reduction.name = "umap_deeptcr")
+
+DimPlot(scRep_example, reduction = "umap_esm", group.by = "clustcr_TRB") +
+  ggtitle("ESM-2") + NoLegend()
+DimPlot(scRep_example, reduction = "umap_deeptcr", group.by = "clustcr_TRB") +
+  ggtitle("DeepTCR VAE") + NoLegend()
+```
+
+The VAE needs enough unique sequences to learn anything meaningful. On a few hundred sequences the latent space is dominated by noise, and the reconstruction accuracy printed during training is the quickest way to see it.
