+## Clonal Expansion Prediction with scXpand
+
+scXpand predicts T-cell clonal expansion from gene expression alone using pretrained pan-cancer models. Unlike the other wrapped tools it needs no receptor sequences, so it works on datasets with no paired TCR sequencing.
+
+List the available models without building the Python environment:
+
+```{r}
+listScXpandModels()
+```
+
+Run inference with the default pan-cancer autoencoder. This writes a predicted probability and a thresholded call back to cell metadata:
+
+```{r}
+scRep_example <- runScXpand(
+  scRep_example,
+  model_name = "pan_cancer_autoencoder"
+)
+
+hist(scRep_example$scXpand_expansion_prob,
+     breaks = 50,
+     main = "Predicted Expansion Probability",
+     xlab = "scXpand_expansion_prob")
+
+table(scRep_example$scXpand_expansion_pred, useNA = "ifany")
+```
+
+When scRepertoire clone calls are present, `runScXpand()` also derives observed clone sizes and an expansion label using scXpand's 1.5x-median rule. That lets a gene-expression-only prediction be scored against the observed repertoire:
+
+```{r}
+df <- data.frame(
+  prob  = scRep_example$scXpand_expansion_prob,
+  truth = scRep_example$scXpand_expansion_truth
+)
+df <- df[!is.na(df$truth), ]
+
+boxplot(prob ~ truth, data = df,
+        main = "Predicted probability vs observed expansion",
+        xlab = "Observed expansion", ylab = "scXpand_expansion_prob")
+```
+
+Note the model is indexed by Ensembl IDs. If your object uses gene symbols, `runScXpand()` maps them through `org.Hs.eg.db` and reports ambiguous symbols and collapsed duplicates rather than resolving them silently, because scXpand zero-fills genes it cannot find.
