+# Handing Off to Python: scanpy and scirpy
+
+`exportToScanpy()` writes the object out for the Python single-cell ecosystem. It accepts a `SingleCellExperiment` or a `Seurat` object and handles the conversion internally.
+
+Writing gene expression alone produces an H5AD file plus an AIRR rearrangement TSV sidecar:
+
+```{r}
+res <- exportToScanpy(
+  scRep_example,
+  output_dir = "scanpy_export",
+  format     = "h5ad",
+  write_airr = TRUE
+)
+
+res$h5ad
+res$airr
+```
+
+Writing `h5mu` instead assembles gene expression and receptor data into a single MuData file that scirpy can read directly, with chains already indexed:
+
+```{r}
+res <- exportToScanpy(
+  scRep_example,
+  output_dir  = "scanpy_export",
+  format      = "h5mu",
+  chains      = "both",
+  reductions  = c("tcr_esm", "tcr_deeptcr"),
+  obs_columns = c("clustcr_TRB", "symdel_degree")
+)
+```
+
+On the Python side that file loads with no further preparation:
+
+```{python, eval = FALSE}
+import muon as mu
+import scirpy as ir
+
+mdata = mu.read_h5mu("scanpy_export/scRep_example.h5mu")
+ir.tl.chain_qc(mdata)
+```
+
+BCR loci (`IGH`, `IGK`, `IGL`) are mapped to the heavy and light chains that `immApex::getIR()` expects and disambiguated by V-gene prefix, so B-cell data exports the same way.
